movie studios had a history with comic  book movies by the year 2000  unfortunately with a few exceptions that  wasn't a very good history the pattern  for super heroic films over the last  five decades had been a cycle of  originality followed by shoddy imitators  and sequels followed by a break followed  by the whole thing starting over again  things changed in 2000 with the release  of x-men 2 both critical acclaim and  commercial success closed on his heels  came Sam Raimi's spider-man in 2002  which was the third highest-grossing  film of the year and sent Hollywood  scrambling to adapt every comic property  they could get their hands on 2003 's x2  and 2004 spider-man to up the ante  critically beloved in commercial  blockbusters these two set the bar for  the first five years of the new  millennium after half a decade of fun  flashy comic bear and also the movie  Catwoman the industry got a shock in  2005 with the release of Batman Begins  followed in the same year by V for  Vendetta  and since it gritty realism adult themes  and no more spandex in primary colors  Batman Begins set a new standard for  everything that followed it if that  didn't cement it enough the critical  battering that Bryan Singer's Superman  Returns to the following year confirmed  that audiences didn't want to throw back  to a more innocent age the darkness of  high grossing superhero Pictures  affected non comic movies as well from  Star Trek to Skyfall blockbusters have  spent a decade imitating the trends that  started in this genre the next  game-changer was in 2008 when the Dark  Knight earned half a billion dollars  domestically making it the highest  grossing film of the year and raised the  standards for all superhero movies Heath  Ledger's terrifying performances the  Joker earned him a posthumous Oscar and  helps make the film an inescapable  phenomenon that summer this was the  height of the dark superhero grip no  other film ever did serious as well but  it didn't stop them from trying the  second-place film of 2008 seemed like a  long shot starring an actor who spent  some time out of the limelight in recent  years and directed by the man who had  most recently made satura Iron Man  defied expectations and delivered an  incredible blend of smart funny and  exciting that would come to define the  Marvel Cinematic Universe that followed  it there was another 2008 entry in  Marvel's movie  the world the Incredible Hulk but rather  than the fun of Ironman it honed closer  to the scowly seriousness that dominated  comic movies at the time and is pretty  much forgotten subsequent marvel  pictures have all been fashioned roughly  in the ironman model while warner  brothers and DC collaborations have all  had a certain dark and gritty streak  even extending it to everybody's  favorite Big Blue Boy Scout Superman in  2013 it's arguable that nothing's had  the same impact on the world of comic  movies subsequent to the launch of the  Marvel Series in the six years since  it's become seemingly a requirement to  have every movie of Studio makes be an  entry into a larger world with expanded  plots and characters shared across film  DC Warner Brothers and Fox and Stoney  owned Marvel titles have all attempted  to bottle the same magic that Disney  owned Marvel perfected with 2012's  Avengers which significantly beat The  Dark Knight Rises that is seemingly  proving the shift away from standalone  films but why have we seen a solid  decade and a half of uninterrupted super  heroics on the big screen the credit is  probably due in part to the technical  breakthroughs that have allowed these  movies to be as visually rich and  believably fantastic as they're drawn  comic counterparts in addition the  business has changed since the comic  bubbles of the 80s and 90s giant media  corporations now approach every movie as  a franchise look for stories that come  pre-loaded with proven super fans and  own both movie studios and the  publishing companies that produce cons  with Disney and Marvel planning a  rollout of films through at least 2021  and Warner Brothers Fox and Sony all  slating superhero films well into the  next decade this kind of movie shows no  sign of slowing down so get used to the  masks and capes it's been a long road  but it looks like they're here to stay  what's your favorite comic movie which  comics would you like to see in movie  form comment below and let us know how  you feel
 

